---
import TechniquePost from "../../layouts/TechniquePost.astro"
import DescriptionListBuild from "../../components/UnderTheHood/DescriptionListBuild.astro"
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components"
---

<TechniquePost slug="description-list-leader-lines">
  <section>
    <h2 id="the-problem">The problem</h2>
    <p>
      Spec sheets, price lists and receipts put the term on the left, the value
      on the right and a dotted line in between. The old ways are a row of dots
      clipped with <code>overflow: hidden</code>, a repeating background, or an
      empty filler element.
    </p>
    <p>
      Side by side also only works when there is room, and a media query knows
      the viewport, not the column the list sits in.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="the-trick">The trick</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Each <code>&lt;dt&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;dd&gt;</code> pair is wrapped in
        a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code>, which is valid in a
        <code>&lt;dl&gt;</code>, so every pair is its own grid
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>container-type: inline-size</code> on the list: wider than
        <code>45ch</code>, term and description share a row
      </li>
      <li>
        The pair's <code>::after</code> is a grid item too.
        <code>order: 1</code> puts it between the term and the description, and the
        <code>1fr</code> column stretches it
      </li>
      <li>
        Its bottom border is the line. The dotted variant only swaps the border
        style and width
      </li>
    </ul>
    <Code
      lang="css"
      code={`.list {
  container-type: inline-size;
}

@container (width > 45ch) {
  .item {
    align-items: baseline;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: auto 1fr auto;
  }

  .item::after {
    block-size: 2px;
    border-block-end: 2px dotted var(--border-color);
    content: "";
    order: 1;
  }

  .item dd {
    order: 2;
  }
}`}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="try-it">Try it</h2>
    <DescriptionListBuild />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="fallback">Fallback</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Without container queries the <code>@container</code> block is skipped: every
        pair stacks, term above description, with no line
      </li>
      <li>
        That is also the layout in a narrow container, so an old browser and a
        narrow column look the same
      </li>
      <li>
        The line is an empty pseudo-element, so assistive tech still reads term,
        then description
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</TechniquePost>
